Date: Fri, 19 Feb 1993 18:48:45 -0800
From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Subject: LAW: "initiating force"

Scott DeLancey is leading us all a merry chase by reformulating his
assertions whenever it's apparent that sufficient counterexamples have been
presented to refute the previous one.  Originally, his statement was that
ownership of territory is purely socially-constructed.  When sufficient
counter-examples were presented, demonstrating that non-verbal species
(specifically dogs and seagulls) indeed demarcate and defend territory, he
then claimed that what he meant to say was that _individual_ ownership of
territory _in social species_ is purely socially-constructed.

Is the latter assertion even true?  If true, is it relevant?

I submit that this most recent formulation is a tautology -- by definition,
social species in the wild exhibit collective ownership of territory.  My
Random House Unabridged (1987 edition) gives the zoological definition of
"social" as "living habitually together in communities, as bees or ants." 
As will become relevant in a moment, RHU87 also gives as the zoological
antonym of social, "solitary" -- "living habitually alone or in pairs, as
certain wasps."

Evidence does indicate that humans in the wild (before being domesticated)
did live in packs, as do wild dogs, wolves, and other primates.  Other
species, notably the big cats, eagles, and most songbirds, 

All of the aforementioned species do exhibit territoriality, however.  A
wolf who trespasses on the territory of another wolf pack will be driven
off or torn apart by its members.  (See Lorenz, _On Aggression_.)  Bees
defend their territory most effectively.  So, it's clear that ownership of
territory, as indicated by the inclination to defend it from depredation,
is a behavior exhibited by many species in the wild.

What is the social unit of ownership in these communities?  The members of
the "in-group" which owns the land are all _highly consanguinous_ -- the
members of a wolf pack, the bees of a hive, and the lions in a pride are
all bound by mating relations.  The concept of the "alpha male", who enjoys
_droit de signeur_ upon all females of the pack, leaving the rest of the
males out in the cold, is a familiar one to anyone who has so much as
watched a _National Geographic_ special.  (Similar is the reproductive
monopoly enjoyed by the queen bee of a hive.)  So, while these social
species do congregate in groups and hold territory in common, the unit of
commonality is at the level of a _family_ -- one or more breeding pairs,
their siblings, and offspring.

Do humans living under liberty do otherwise?  Even the staunchest defender
of the right of individual property typically shares his territory, food,
and other resources with his mating partners and their offspring.  Does
this invalidate his ownership?  If, returning to Scott's original example,
Scott now walks across the territory of this "pack", he is in fact
trespassing in a very real way that pre-dates civilization and "social
context", and, like any wolf pack, beehive, or pair of nesting starlings,
these owners in particular are likely to see to it that he buzzes off.

Does this same relationship -- the family as unit of land ownership --
obtain for humans living in the wild?  Unfortunately, we have no first-hand
data on undomesticated humans, since all humans now living on the planet
exhibit some signs of domestication, i.e., civilization:  language usage,
the employment of fire, and so forth.  Scott cites several examples (some
first-hand) of human civilizations that extend the unit of territorial
ownership to the tribe.  Note that these civilizations exist(ed) in regions
of material plenty, where the supply of good fishing sites (and fish)
consistently exceeds the demand.  (As a side note, purely out of curiosity,
I wonder how they manage to keep the demand down?  High infant mortality,
I'd guess -- in which case, what happens to the economies of these tribes
once they get Western vaccines and antibiotics, and more children grow to
adulthood?  If it's not death from disease, then what keeps the population
down?  Infanticide isn't unheard of in many parts of the world.)

Scott sez:
>     Nowadays people have built houses in communities mostly located
>near old fishing sites.  But, at least in communities I have been in,
>there is still no institution of private ownership of land.  Anyone
>can, and does, build a house anywhere they like.  Once built, the
>*house* is recognized as family property, but there is still no
>associated ownership of land--just because you have built a house,
>that does not give you (and as far as the community is concerned,
>nothing ever would give you) any right to interfere with any other
>member of the community, no matter what they are doing or where.

(Sorry for the extended quote, but I want to ensure that I'm not taking
Scott out of -- if you'll pardon the expression -- context.)

>.. no matter what they are doing or where. [!?]

If another member of the tribe walks into these folks' living room and
defecates on the rug, do the inhabitants blithely condede that that's
perfectly okay?  How can one build a house on a piece of land, and claim
not to be interfering with others' use of the plot of ground underneath it?
 If that particular plot happened to be the traditional shitting-ground
used by that tribe when camped out at this particular lake, then the family
who built the house there have to allow the fellow to do his business right
on the rug, or else they are claiming a "right to interfere with [another]
member of the community, no matter what they are doing or where."

In any case, the presentation of any number of counterexamples of this type
does not prove that "individual ownership of land is a social construct"
any more than it proves "collective ownership of land is a social
construct".  The adducing of any behavior observed within a social context
is irrelevant to the demonstration of whether or not that behavior is a
social construct.  To put this in kindergarten terms that we can all
understand, if I tell you that I saw Joe driving in the rain, it indicates
nothing about the truth or falsity of the proposition "Joe only drives when
it's raining."  To prove or disprove this assertion, we would have to
observe Joe when it is _not_ raining.  Even if I have seen Joe drive in the
rain on many, many occasions, it still says nothing about Joe's driving
behavior under non-rainy conditions.

I will add in closing that I have filed a complaint with the list
administrator about Scott's patronizing tone and offensive
superciliousness, as evidenced by this quote (in which Scott quotes
Krzys'):

>> Please state your purpose and explain argument. 
>
>Purpose?  Argument?  My purpose is simply to correct ignorance, and
>my argument is nothing more than a presentation of facts.

It is my happy duty on this occasion to inform Scott that among the
subscribership of this list, he is not the sole possessor of facts, and
that the presentation of _relevant_ facts might do more to advance his
viewpoint.  If his object is truly to inform, rather than merely to raise
hackles, I suggest that he adopt a style of discourse that is more oriented
toward the systematic presentation of argument.
