In what follows, I praise Scott DeLancey, criticize his response to my
challenge, and ultimately conclude that our dispute over the facts makes
little difference.

I salute Scott for responding to my challenge that he append citations in
support of his claims about property rights among animals and in primitive
human societies.  I was hoping he would quote some of these sources, much
as I quoted Hayek.  I guess that is asking a lot, though.  

Scott was, at least, gracious enough to admit that 

>there's a
>couple of these I haven't read, and what I have read was quite a
>while ago, . . .

While at first I thought it was odd that Scott was supporting his claims by
citing sources that he hasn't read or has largely forgotten, I charitably
concluded that he was simply encouraging me to pursue a counter-challenge. 
That's rather sporting, I think.

I can't say the same about this exchange:

>> I believe it is contradicted by the available data.

>You're wrong.  Pure and simple.  If you disagree, show me some of the
>"available data".

I hardly know whether to regard Scott's conviction with amusement or alarm!
 Do you really believe, Scott, that ethology and anthropology are hard
enough sciences to support your faith in "pure and simple" conclusions?  I
can understand having an opinion about the strength of the various theories
(hence my careful phrasing about my belief about available data), but your
earnestness strikes me as either charmingly naive or sadly doctrinaire.  

Such hard and fast conviction can come back to haunt you.  I don't think
you have succeeded in supporting your claims about individual ownership of
land.  You originally said:

>among wild social canines, social primates, and
>humans in hunter-gatherer societies, there is no such thing as individual
>land ownership, even in the ethological sense of defended territory.  

You later followed up with cites to canines and chimps.  But you didn't say
anything more in support of the most interesting and controversial part of
your claim, that part relating to land ownership in hunter-gatherer
societies.  I need find only one example to cast doubt on your ambitious
claim that "there is no such thing as . . .".  Here it is:

The Yurok, Hupa, and Karok tribes that occupied what is now Northern
California subsisted on salmon and whatever else they could glean from the
surrounding forests.  Yet they recognized individual rights to geographic
territory--mostly fishing places.  They had a quite sophisticated system
for establishing and adjudicating claims to such real property.  See Walter
Goldsmidt, "Ethics and the Structure of Society:  An Ethnological
Contribution to the Sociology of Knowledge," 53 AMERICAN ANTHROPOLOGIST 506
(October/December 1951).

As you so pungently put it yourself, Scott, 

>It's a shame to let a few facts get in the way of a perfectly good
>theory, eh?

Though I think that the evidence I have cited deflates your overly-broad
claim about the universality of real property rights, I also think that you
have a valid point.  I should have read your intial claim more carefully;
you were not attacking property rights per se, but only the idea that all
types of social animals and human societies recognize individual claims to
real property.  You could have supported this claim by giving merely *one*
example of a human society that ignored real property.  You mentioned
Eskimos as one such society.  I'll second the example with Comanches, who
defended individual personal property with great ferocity while virtually
ignoring real property rights.  See E. Adamson Hoebel, "Law-Ways of the
Comanche Indians," in LAW AND WARFARE, Paul Bohanan, ed., 188 (Garden City,
NY: The Natural History Press, 1967).

But I conclude that none of this seems to make any important difference in
how we regard property rights.  The deep principle is that customary legal
systems come to protect scarce resources.  Land is very, very often such a
resource; especially in farming cultures (such as the Ifuago of Northern
Luzon) but sometimes also in hunter-gatherer ones (such as the Yuroks). 
Sometimes, however, there is so much land of so little value that a
customary legal system ignores rights to it (see the Comanche).  

These variations on a common theme tell us nothing about the validity of
individual real property rights, though.  Finding a culture that recognized
*no* individual property rights *would*.  I have never heard of such a
culture, and thus stick to my working hypothesis that we have a
deep--perhaps even inborn--desire to own property.  What specific kind of
property we end up recognizing, will depend on what we need to survive and
what we find hard to get.


