From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: LAW: "initiating force"

Carol Moore asked about Rupert Sheldrake's theory of "morphogenetic
fields".  Sheldrake believes that once a phenomenon occurs once in
the universe, that makes it easier for the phenomenon to recur.
For example, once a difficult organic chemical is synthesized, future
efforts to synthesize that same chemical would be easier.  Similarly,
once a certain number of people learn a language, that language will
be easier for people to learn in the future.

You can even use this theory to explain the very existance of
natural laws: energy is conserved, for example, because it has been
conserved so often in the past and the universe has fallen into a
practically unbreakable habit.

Sheldrake's theories are generally considered pseudoscience.  I believe
there were some tests in the Skeptical Enquirer a few years ago which
failed to replicate his results.  If they were true, though, they
would offer many new ways to manipulate the universe.  But I wouldn't
bet on any of it.

