Subject: ATM: ATM defined

Tim May asks about the what ATM is, why I posted about it, etc.

ATM is a form of switching technology which is ideally built from
a silcon based banyan switching fabric.

It supports multiple channels at the various OC (Optical Carrier) levels.
OC-1 is ~50 Megabits per sec., OC-3 is ~150 Megabits per sec., OC-12
is ~600 Megabits per sec. IT GOES UP TO OC-24.

2 years ago ATM was 5 years away. Today it is here! Sprint and AT&T
are building ATM networks.

ATM can be used on both the desktop level and on the wide area level.
The article posted talked about using ATM switching fabric within
a desktop computer.

I have commented on ATM on this list many times. Time-Warner is using
ATM in Flordia to provide various digital, interactive, analog, and
bi-directional services through Fiber and Coax. These include NTSC
video, video on demand and telco by-pass.

We should be intersted in ATM because it is so much more powerful than
ISDN (which is promoted by Al Gore, et al)

ATM is a form of Cell Rely which is a will be a standard mode of
transport under ISO's B-ISDN (Broadband ISDN).

ATM stands for Asynchronous Transfer Mode.

Any extropian who readS any data communcation mags, cable TV mags, or
even the general press should have heard of ATM. Given my previous
discussion of it on this list I consider it to be common knowledge.



