From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Robin and Tim have both posted about Thomas M. Cover's writings on
economics and finance.  I haven't been able to find the book Tim
mentioned (it's checked out at one and missing at the other Univ.
library that I have access to).  But I found a few of Cover's papers
which had some very interesting results.

One paper had some results which I found amazingly paradoxical.  Suppose
there is an investment, say a stock, which in each period will either
double in value or lose half its value.  If these happened with 50-50
likelihood, then an investment in this stock would have no net gain over
a period of time.  This points out the importance of looking at "log
return"; a factor of 2 and a factor of 1/2 are equal and opposite in
log terms.

Now, let's suppose that instead of 50-50 probability, the factor of 2
increase happens 60% of the time, and the factor of 1/2 happens 40% of
the time.  The stock now has a positive average return, which can be
calculated as exp (.60*log(2) + .40*log(.5)).  You average the log returns
with weights of .60 and .40.  This works out to be about a 14.9% return
each period.

Now for the interesting part.  Suppose you have some money which you can
put into either this stock and/or just keep some in cash.  Cash offers no
return, while the stock offers this 14.9% return.  How much should you
put in each to maximize your average return?  I would have thought you
should put 100% into the stock since it has a higher average return
than the cash.

But Cover shows that this is not optimal.  Actually, for this case the
optimal strategy is to put 80% of your money into the stock and keep 20%
in cash.  Then, after each period (where the stock has either doubled or
halved) you redistribute your money to keep 80% in the stock and 20% in
cash.

If you do this, then for each period in which the stock doubles your
money goes up by (.20 + .80*2) or 1.8; for each period in which the stock
halves your money gets multiplied by (.20 + .80*.5) or .6.  Your average
return, calculated as above, is exp (.60*log(1.8) + .40*log(.6)) (compare
this with the formula above).  This works out to be about a 16.0% return
each period, which is _higher_ than the 14.9% return you get if you just
keep everything in the stock!

So, even though the stock has a higher average return than the cash, you
do better over the long run to keep 20% of your money in cash, and to
rebalance your portfolio to maintain this fraction.  I find this to be a
remarkably non-intuitive result!

Cover has some other good stuff in these papers.  I got these numbers
from his "Empirical Bayes Stock Market Portfolios", Advances in Applied
Mathematics vol 7, p 170-181, 1986.  Here is the abstract:

"We exhibit a compound sequential Bayes portfolio selection algorithm
based solely on the past which not only lives off market fluctuations but
follows the drift as well.  In fact, this sequential portfolio performs
as well (up to first order terms in the exponent) as the optimal
portfolio based on advance knowledge of the n-period empirical
distribution of the market.  Moreover, to first order in the exponent,
the capital resulting from this portfolio will be no less than the best
of the available stocks.  This is a result that holds for every sample
sequence.  Thus bull markets and bear markets can not fool the investor
into over-committing or under-committing his capital to the risky
alternatives available to him.  The goal is accomplished by a choice of
portfolio which is robust with respect to futures that may differ
drastically from the past."

These are pretty strong claims, and I am still trying to understand them
from the paper.  I'm not sure they can be applied in practice to real
markets.

Hal

